WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.978
Want to see a result first?

00:00:01.978 --> 00:00:06.778
In the Korean or English readme, click the link for the
example report zip.

00:00:06.778 --> 00:00:11.130
You can open it on your own computer, without installing
NanoCoFold.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:14.317
Extract the whole zip into one folder.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:17.119
You can use Extract All in your file manager.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:18.178
Here I use unzip.

00:00:18.178 --> 00:00:19.673
Keep the files together.

00:00:22.250 --> 00:00:23.940
Open report dot H T M L.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.109
When the table appears, choose your language.

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:31.687
This saved example has eleven evaluated models and zero
selected.

00:00:31.687 --> 00:00:33.941
The calculation still completed.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:36.815
The summary switches to English.

00:00:36.815 --> 00:00:41.009
Select a candidate to rotate its structure and see the
error map.

00:00:41.009 --> 00:00:42.945
Everything here opens offline.

00:00:42.945 --> 00:00:45.139
Next we will run a new experiment.

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:02.813
To run your own prediction, use an Ubuntu computer with an
NVIDIA driver.

00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:07.077
In the terminal, check that nvidia-smi lists your graphics
card.

00:01:07.077 --> 00:01:09.342
This one is an R T X thirty sixty.

00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:13.763
Download the tagged public release, then enter its folder.

00:01:13.763 --> 00:01:17.750
For a new installation, use the current tag shown in the
readme.

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:21.051
For an existing installation, follow its update link.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:24.541
First check the host.

00:01:24.541 --> 00:01:30.034
For this particular Boltz two exercise, the screen profile
provides the tools we need.

00:01:30.034 --> 00:01:33.483
System packages were already installed on this server.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:37.423
Leave the terminal open while the models download.

00:01:37.423 --> 00:01:40.795
This is a real installation; the video skips the wait.

00:01:40.795 --> 00:01:43.793
Continue when the installer says it is finished.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.055
Run start dot sh in the same folder.

00:01:47.055 --> 00:01:51.828
Open the address printed after NanoCoFold and leave the
terminal open.

00:01:51.828 --> 00:01:56.397
For a shared server, use the connection instructions in the
readme.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.435
Download the practice input zip from the readme.

00:02:00.435 --> 00:02:01.177
Extract it.

00:02:01.177 --> 00:02:05.625
Use these files as they are: one structure, two protein
sequences,

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:08.187
and a table of binding-site positions.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.215
Open the Fasta files as text.

00:02:11.215 --> 00:02:13.278
The first line is the name.

00:02:13.278 --> 00:02:15.952
The letters below are the sequence.

00:02:15.952 --> 00:02:17.632
E G F R is the target.

00:02:17.632 --> 00:02:21.757
Seven D twelve is the original nanobody, or wild type.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.731
Choose the option to improve an existing nanobody.

00:02:25.731 --> 00:02:28.861
Give the analysis a name you will recognize later.

00:02:28.861 --> 00:02:32.243
We will use this same name to find it in the database.

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:44.031
Choose structure input and load four K R L dot cif.

00:02:44.031 --> 00:02:46.950
Choose the E G F R chain, B in this menu.

00:02:46.950 --> 00:02:49.015
Clear the partner chain here.

00:02:49.015 --> 00:02:51.792
We will supply the nanobody separately.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:03.883
Paste the supplied E G F R target Fasta.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:07.093
Choose the option to enter reference positions.

00:03:07.093 --> 00:03:09.621
Enter nineteen, forty, and forty-two.

00:03:09.621 --> 00:03:12.969
These are positions in the input target sequence.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:26.153
The numbering table links those positions to the original
structure: three twenty-five,

00:03:26.153 --> 00:03:28.486
three forty-six, and three forty-eight.

00:03:28.486 --> 00:03:31.297
Enter only the binding sites you want to study.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:45.497
Confirm the coordinates and paste the original nanobody
Fasta.

00:03:45.497 --> 00:03:47.110
Move to the settings step.

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:51.143
We are changing the nanobody; the target sequence stays the
same.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.836
Choose random substitutions in C D R three, a loop of the
nanobody.

00:04:01.836 --> 00:04:03.321
Request ten variants.

00:04:03.321 --> 00:04:07.916
This is ten different sequences, not ten changes in one
sequence.

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:13.561
Set both the minimum and maximum substitutions to two.

00:04:13.561 --> 00:04:16.695
Include the unchanged wild type as a control.

00:04:16.695 --> 00:04:20.595
With one engine and one seed, the plan is eleven models.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.506
Review the settings before running.

00:04:35.506 --> 00:04:38.909
Check the ten variants, the wild type, and the engine.

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:44.265
This exercise uses Boltz two without sending sequences to a
public alignment service.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.446
Check the environment.

00:04:46.446 --> 00:04:48.587
This step checks your installation.

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:52.503
When it says Environment ready, return to the reviewed
settings.

00:04:52.503 --> 00:04:54.828
The real analysis has not started yet.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.130
Press Run analysis once.

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.001
Find your analysis name in Jobs and logs.

00:05:10.001 --> 00:05:13.992
Queued means wait; running means the server is computing.

00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:16.863
Do not submit the same job a second time.

00:05:32.450 --> 00:05:37.240
If you already installed NanoCoFold, use the update guide
for Git or zip.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.946
Finish running jobs first.

00:05:38.946 --> 00:05:43.736
In this recording we updated the same installation after
the calculation,

00:05:43.736 --> 00:05:47.149
then reopened its saved result without rerunning it.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.443
The video skips computation time here.

00:05:50.443 --> 00:05:53.921
Once this same job says Complete, click Open results.

00:05:53.921 --> 00:05:57.465
You do not need to find a folder or import the result.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.022
Read the saved selection decision first.

00:06:01.022 --> 00:06:04.289
A completed run can have no eligible candidates.

00:06:04.289 --> 00:06:07.011
The exclusion section gives the reasons.

00:06:07.011 --> 00:06:10.823
A good interface score alone is not evidence of binding.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:16.618
In Compare candidates, select wild type first, then a
mutant from the same engine.

00:06:16.618 --> 00:06:21.101
The candidate name above the viewer tells you which model
you are reading.

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:24.377
Here is the actual V H H sequence.

00:06:24.377 --> 00:06:28.019
Use Copy sequence, or Save this candidate as Fasta.

00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:32.659
That file contains exactly the sequence of the selected
nanobody.

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:39.956
For this one candidate, read i p S A E and i p T M as
measures of confidence in the interface

00:06:39.956 --> 00:06:42.525
and in the arrangement of the chains.

00:06:42.525 --> 00:06:45.788
These are predictions, not measured affinities.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:50.195
Select the nanobody, then drag the structure to rotate it.

00:06:50.195 --> 00:06:55.091
Both the sequence and this three dimensional view belong to
the same candidate.

00:06:55.900 --> 00:07:00.306
The error map shows how uncertain the relative residue
positions are.

00:07:00.306 --> 00:07:02.797
Lower values mean less predicted error.

00:07:02.797 --> 00:07:05.607
Use the axes and the units on the color bar.

00:07:06.450 --> 00:07:10.878
To share this run, choose the interactive report zip and
export it.

00:07:10.878 --> 00:07:15.373
The recipient can extract the whole zip and open report dot
H T M L,

00:07:15.373 --> 00:07:17.223
just as we did at the start.

00:07:18.050 --> 00:07:22.670
Later, open Results database, then Candidate sequences and
structures.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:27.027
Type the analysis name you used at the beginning and click
Search.

00:07:27.027 --> 00:07:29.997
You can also search by candidate or sequence.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.386
Click the i p S A E column heading to sort the table.

00:07:34.386 --> 00:07:36.821
Sorting changes the order on screen.

00:07:36.821 --> 00:07:40.272
It does not change the original selection decision.

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:46.635
Save the search results to Excel, or click a candidate name
to reopen its sequence and

00:07:46.635 --> 00:07:47.279
structure.

00:07:47.279 --> 00:07:52.364
The database, the Fasta file, and the report all point back
to the same result.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:59.078
If your lab shares one installation, the administrator sets
up an account for each researcher.

00:07:59.078 --> 00:08:03.330
Here an administrator changes their own password and signs
in again.

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.019
The passwords stay private.

00:08:05.850 --> 00:08:08.768
By default researchers see their own results.

00:08:08.768 --> 00:08:13.696
The administrator can review all results and change the lab
sharing setting.

00:08:13.696 --> 00:08:19.079
A researcher uses the account and connection details
supplied by the lab; no second

00:08:19.079 --> 00:08:20.570
installation is needed.
